window.docContents[130].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Modified-Helmholtz-Equation--The-role-of-the-positive-mass-term":{"contents":"The homogeneous-Dirichlet modified Helmholtz problem is\n\n\n\nIts weak form pairs gradients and adds the mass term\n.  When , the two terms directly control\nthe full graph-space norm: the gradient controls the derivative coordinates\nand the mass term controls the base  coordinate.  This is why coercivity\nholds without boundedness of the domain or a Poincaré inequality.\n\nThere are two levels.  On an arbitrary set, the bilinear form\nand its Lax–Milgram solver are functional-analytic objects.  On an open\nset, compactly supported smooth functions are available as PDE test\nfunctions, so the same solution can be read distributionally.  Density of\nthose tests in  makes the two formulations equivalent.\n\nBesides positive , we treat a signed regime, in which a negative\nmass contribution is allowed whenever its size is smaller than an available\nDirichlet coercivity modulus.\n\nOn , the modified Helmholtz form isIt is defined for every set , measurable or not.\n\nFor , on every set  and in every\ndimension, the form  is coercive on : there is a\nconstant  with\n\nExpanding  gives the squared gradient norm plus\n times the squared base  norm. Each coefficient is bounded\nbelow by , so their sum controls that constant times the\nfull graph-space norm squared;  is the constant the\nproof supplies.\n\nWhen  is negative the mass term works against coercivity, and a\nlower bound can only come from the gradient term, through a Dirichlet\ncoercivity modulus. On bounded domains of positive dimension, Poincaré's\ninequality supplies one.\n\nSuppose the Dirichlet form has a coercivity modulus :If the negative part of the mass coefficient satisfies , then\n\nWrite .  The positive part can be discarded.\nThe base-coordinate norm is bounded by the full graph-space norm, so the\nnegative mass contribution is bounded below by\n.  Subtracting this from the Dirichlet\nlower bound leaves the positive coefficient .\n\nThe signed result gives coercivity, hence unique solvability through\nLax–Milgram. The named solver, and every result built on it, is stated for\n. It is the Lax–Milgram solution for the  load\n.\n\nFor  and , the named solver is the unique\n satisfyingSatisfying this weak equation is equivalent to equality with the named\nsolver.\n\nOn every domain and for , the solver sends zero data\nto zero.  Moreover, there is a constant , depending on the fixed form,\nsuch that for all ,\n\nThe zero-data identity follows from uniqueness.  Boundedness of the\nLax–Milgram solution operator gives the first estimate for some positive\nconstant depending only on the fixed form.  Apply the same estimate to\n and use linearity of the solver to obtain the Lipschitz bound.\n\nThe form  is symmetric, so the weak equation admits a variational\ncharacterization. The functional is the Dirichlet functional with the mass\nterm added to its quadratic part.\n\nFor any , define\n\nIf , every weak solution minimizes\n.  If , coercivity makes the characterization\nexact:The named solution  is the unique minimizer, and every other\nfield has strictly larger energy.\n\nSymmetry and nonnegativity expand the energy difference around a weak\nsolution into one half of .  For nonnegative\n this proves minimality.  For positive , coercivity makes\nthe difference vanish only when , giving the converse, strictness, and\nuniqueness.\n\nEverything so far has been Hilbert-space theory on an arbitrary set. To read\n as the PDE , assume\n is open and test against compactly supported smooth functions. The raw\nsmooth-test identity makes sense on arbitrary sets; the solution predicate\nrequires openness.\n\nOn an open domain, the distributional predicate requires\n\nThe variational and distributional formulations differ only in their test\nclasses, and the canonical image of  is dense in\n. The equivalence therefore holds for every real ,\ncoercive or not; the sign of  matters only for solvability.\n\nOn an open domain,The right-hand side is the compactly supported smooth-test formulation.\n\nOne direction evaluates the Hilbert-space identity on canonical smooth tests.\nFor the reverse direction, density of smooth compactly supported tests in\n and continuity of both sides extend equality to every Sobolev test.\n\nCombining the equivalence with the coercive Lax–Milgram theory transports\nexistence and uniqueness to the distributional side.\n\nOn every open domain, if , each\n admits exactly one  satisfying\n\nConstruct the coercive Lax–Milgram solution, transfer its weak equation to\nthe distributional predicate, and use the same equivalence plus uniqueness of\nthe coercive Hilbert-space problem for any competing solution.\n\nUniqueness can be sharpened to an identification: any distributional solution\nmust coincide with the named solver. This is the form in which the result is\nmost useful, since it converts statements about the PDE into statements about\na single operator.\n\nOn an open domain with , a distributional solution is\nthe named variational solution:For zero forcing this specializes to .\n\nThe distributional equivalence turns the PDE predicate into the variational\nweak equation.  The solver characterization and uniqueness of the coercive\nproblem then identify  with .  For zero forcing, combine\nthis equivalence with the solver's zero-data identity.\n\nLinearity of the distributional predicate in the pair  holds for every\nreal , since it is a property of the integral identity alone.\nThrough the identification with the named solver it becomes linearity of\n for .\n\nDistributional modified Helmholtz solutions are closed under addition,\nnegation, subtraction, and real scalar multiplication, with the forcing data\ntransformed by the same operation.  Consequently, for ,\n\nLinearity of the integral and gradient identities proves closure of the\ndistributional predicate under the listed operations.  When ,\nthe characterization by the unique named solver turns those closure results\ninto the two displayed solver identities.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Modified Helmholtz Equation","header":"5.4.3.1. The role of the positive mass term","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Modified-Helmholtz-Equation--The-role-of-the-positive-mass-term"},"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis","header":"3.4.1. Canonical distributions","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Sharper-Enclosures-and-the-Precision-Option":{"contents":"Four mechanisms help sharpen a certified range. Products and argument\nreduction improve interval evaluation; derivative enclosures and precision\nchoices provide further ways to tighten it. The planner compares available\nroutes, and every accepted enclosure carries its own soundness proof.\n\nProducts. Interval multiplication is the exact four-corner product: for\n and  the product lies in the hull of , ,\n, and . For  on\n a symmetric magnitude bound would give ; the four\ncorners give the sharp range.\n\n-- Two coordinates, one exact product.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [-1, 2], y in [2, 3]] (x * y)\n\n\n#cas [enclose x in [-1, 2], y in [2, 3]]\nDomain: x ∈ [-1, 2], y ∈ [2, 3] ⊆ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar map ℝ^2 → ℝ): (x * y)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [-3, 6]\nUpper bound: 6\nAbsolute bound: 6\nCenter and radius: 3/2 ± 9/2\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nArgument reduction. FloatLib supplies sine and cosine enclosures with\ncertified period reduction and midpoint bounds. The local adapter uses degree\n and refines those bounds using proved monotonicity and derivative-sign\nconditions. A narrow panel far from the origin can receive a tighter enclosure\nthan the global range :\n\n-- A narrow panel around x = 10.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [10, 1001 / 100]] (sin x)\n\n\n#cas [enclose x in [10, 1001 / 100]]\nDomain: x ∈ [10, 1001/100] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (sin x)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [-0.552385, -0.544021]\nUpper bound: -0.544021\nAbsolute bound: 0.552385\nCenter and radius: ≈-0.548203 ± 0.004182\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe exact range on this panel is , approximately\n. On  a certified cosine enclosure\nis , and on  a certified sine enclosure is\n; on  the sine enclosure is .\nWhen exact endpoints are long, the headline shows a six-place decimal preview\nthat rounds lower endpoints down and upper endpoints up; the kernel-checked\nproposition keeps the exact rationals.\n\nThe centered form. When an expression repeats a coordinate, the natural\ninterval extension overestimates. If a partial derivative keeps one sign on\nthe box, the monotonicity route replaces the box by its two faces in that\ncoordinate and takes the hull of their enclosures. Each face reruns the\nplanner, so reductions can continue while fuel and fixed-sign derivative\ncertificates remain available. The centered form also competes for selection\nand allows derivative enclosures of either sign. The mean value theorem in\ncentered form,  with  on the\nsegment from the midpoint  to , encloses  by\n; both routes work on boxes of any\nliteral dimension. For example,  on  is certified to its exact\nrange , and\n on  to . In one\nvariable the centered form is . For  on \nthe derivative  changes sign; the natural extension\ngives  and the centered form gives\n\n-- The derivative changes sign, so the\n-- centered form replaces monotonicity.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [9 / 10, 11 / 10]] (x * exp (-x))\n\n\n#cas [enclose x in [9 / 10, 11 / 10]]\nDomain: x ∈ [9/10, 11/10] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (x * exp (-x))\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [0.356443, 0.379316]\nUpper bound: 0.379316\nAbsolute bound: 0.379316\nCenter and radius: ≈0.367879 ± 0.011437\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nagainst the true range, approximately . The planner previews\nthe natural, monotonicity, and centered routes without proofs, then attempts\ncertificate construction in order of previewed sharpness until a route\nsucceeds. The preview determines which route to try; the returned certificate\njustifies the enclosure.\n\nPrecision. The elementary-function ranges are parameterized: a FloatLib degree\nfor the exponential, a series-term request for the logarithm, a number of\nbinary digits for square-root brackets, and a bisection depth for checked\nsubdivision. A Precision value bundles them, with defaults ,\n, , and . The configuration group (prec := n) sets the first\nthree to  and (depth := n) sets the depth; every range theorem is proved\nfor all parameter values, so the policy affects sharpness and cost but never\nsoundness. With the default order,\n\n-- FloatLib degree eight.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [0, 1]] (exp x)\n\n\n#cas [enclose x in [0, 1]]\nDomain: x ∈ [0, 1] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (exp x)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [1, 2.718282]\nUpper bound: 2.718282\nAbsolute bound: 2.718282\nCenter and radius: ≈1.859140 ± 0.859142\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nalso certifies the coarser bound\n, while\n\n-- FloatLib degree forty.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas (prec := 40) [enclose x in [0, 1]] (exp x)\n\n\n#cas (prec := 40) [enclose x in [0, 1]]\nDomain: x ∈ [0, 1] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (exp x)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [1, 2.718282]\nUpper bound: 2.718282\nAbsolute bound: 2.718282\nCenter and radius: ≈1.859140 ± 0.859142\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\ncertifies an upper bound with twenty-four decimal places behind the six-place\npreview: the theorem-facing form\ncega (prec := 40) [enclose x in [0, 1]] (exp x) closes an EnclosedOn goal\nwith requested upper bound . Subdivision depth\nworks the same way. For  on , (depth := 2)\ncertifies an upper bound of ; a third level\ngives\n\n-- Three checked bisection levels.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas (depth := 3)\n  [enclose x in [0, 2]] (sin x * cos x)\n\n\n#cas (depth := 3) [enclose x in [0, 2]]\nDomain: x ∈ [0, 2] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (sin x * cos x)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [-0.378402, 0.544012]\nUpper bound: 0.544012\nAbsolute bound: 0.544012\nCenter and radius: ≈0.082805 ± 0.461207\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nagainst the true range, approximately . The trigonometric Taylor\ndegrees are fixed and are not affected by the option. Repeated identical\nqueries in one file reuse the kernel evaluation of the closed query term; the\ncertificate theorem is checked again in every declaration that uses it.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.7. Sharper Enclosures and the Precision Option","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Sharper-Enclosures-and-the-Precision-Option"}});